<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Human evolution</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as anthropogeny, anthropogenesis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Human evolution is the evolutionary history of Homo sapiens, a species of the hominid family of primates, studied through physical and evolutionary anthropology, paleontology and genetics. Primates diverged from other mammals about 85 million years ago, and the extinct biped ancestors of humans separated from the genus Pan, which includes chimpanzees and bonobos, 4–7 million years ago. The genus Homo is evidenced by the appearance of H. habilis, and anatomically modern humans emerged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> approximately 300,000 years ago. Over this history humans developed bipedalism, dexterity and complex language, and modern humans interbred with archaic humans such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denisovan">Denisovans</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Molecular evidence suggests that between 8 and 4 million years ago first the gorillas and then the chimpanzees split off from the line leading to humans. Human DNA is approximately 98.4 per cent identical to that of chimpanzees when comparing single nucleotide polymorphisms. The earliest fossils proposed as hominins are Sahelanthropus, Orrorin and Ardipithecus kadabba, each argued to be a bipedal ancestor but each contested. The genus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australopithecus">Australopithecus</a> evolved in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> around 4 million years ago and became extinct 2 million years ago, with species including Australopithecus anamensis, A. afarensis and A. africanus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The earliest documented representative of the genus Homo is the Ledi jaw, dated 2.75 to 2.8 million years ago. Homo habilis, with a brain of about 600 cm3, was followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homo-erectus">Homo erectus</a>, which had a brain of 800 to 1,100 cm3 and was the first of the hominin line to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Homo heidelbergensis lived from about 800,000 to about 300,000 years ago, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthals</a> lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and Asia from 400,000 to about 28,000 years ago. Homo sapiens emerged in Africa around 300,000 years ago, likely derived from Homo heidelbergensis or a related lineage.</p><p>Among the main changes were bipedalism, a larger brain, a longer infancy and reduced sexual dimorphism. Ulnar opposition, the contact between the thumb and the tip of the little finger, is unique to the genus Homo. Genomes show that modern humans interbred with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denisovan">Denisovans</a>: about 2 per cent of the DNA of all non-African populations is Neanderthal, and 4 to 6 per cent of modern Melanesian genetics is Denisovan. For this reason evolution was not linear but weblike.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus">Carl Linnaeus</a> chose the name Homo for the genus and, with other scientists of his time, considered the great apes humans' closest relatives. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>'s On the Origin of Species of 1859 did not address human evolution, saying only that light would be thrown on the origin of man. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthal</a> remains were found in 1856, but were first claimed to be those of an ill modern human, and Eugène Dubois found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homo-erectus">Homo erectus</a> at Trinil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, in 1891. In 1925 Raymond Dart described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australopithecus">Australopithecus</a> africanus from the Taung Child.</p><p>In the 1960s and 1970s the Leakey family found hundreds of fossils at Olduvai Gorge and Lake Turkana. The genetic revolution began when Vincent Sarich and Allan Wilson measured immunological distances to build a molecular clock. In 1994 Meave Leakey found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australopithecus">Australopithecus</a> anamensis, and in 1995 Tim D. White's Ardipithecus ramidus pushed back the fossil record.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Human evolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
